How to use the template:

template initialises communication with the control node and some threads
input/output/state machine threads are empty and need to be completed
	obviously will likely need to add more threads for your solutions
	
status update thread is complete, as your program runs it will need to update the update Message struct with current data, 
	note this will be a critical section of code so will need to use mutex writing update struct.

	
##Sending message##
	use the following template to send message (can implement as function call or not up to you)
	dont need to use mq_open
		ssize_t n = mq_send(mqs, (char *)&fM, sizeof(fM), 0);				// Send message
		if (n == -1) {														// check for error with msg send
			perror("mq_send");
			return EXIT_FAILURE;
		} 
	fM struct used in example but can replace all instances of fM with another struct for other cases e.g rM
	will send whatever is in the struct specified at the time

##Receiving message## likely to be altered later as universal solution, will work for prototyping tho
	use the following template to rcv message (can implement as function call or not up to you)
	dont need to use mq_open
		n = mq_receive(mqr, rcvBuffer, sizeof(rcvBuffer), NULL);  				//wait for the message (blocks)
		if (n == -1) {															// error with msg rcv
			perror("mq_receive");												// print error
			return EXIT_FAILURE;
		} else if ((size_t)n != sizeof(cM)){									// wrong struct received
			printf("-->unexpected message size\n");								// print error
		} else {																// else msg received correctly
			memcpy(&cM, rcvBuffer, sizeof(cM));									// save rcv values in rM struct
		}
	data will be saved in designated struct, in exmaple its cM but will also need fM one for exmaple
	The receive queue is unique for each worker node so will have unique rcvMessage path (see code)
	Might need a function to decifer struct received, i will look into this.


If your having trouble with anything just msg the group chat before defaulting to AI, I think we should avoid using it where possible to reduce the expected standard of code delivered.

